#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074881} :activity/id #uuid "4bb23d88-5fbe-55d0-9559-771761b8bf07" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050195 :topic/id #uuid "3596110e-f5b8-50d1-ad35-805fc39570c3" :topic/math-academy-id 557} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074970 :tutorial/content "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1.$ This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1.$\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\[math] 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}.$ Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\div 4}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire." :tutorial/id #uuid "0bf127db-09c1-53b5-b2fa-6f6520b032f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2055 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3556c71-dd79-50f3-ae2c-c8fc08b7ffba" :step/math-academy-id 7810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073886 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073570 :question/id #uuid "579bda4d-058c-5572-8d5d-addf9650d1ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-2291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a5b57e9-71fa-5b06-9984-c18e20f79e4e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050481}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490281}] :knowledge-point/